    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d723">

                <pb facs="0929" n="917" sameAs="frus:frus1946v07/pg917"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.51/11–1046: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wilson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-11-10T12:00:00+02:00">November 10,
                            1946—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 3 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1180. Mytel 1174, November 8. In view Prime
                    Minister’s request for additional credits Turkish Navy’s request for credits to
                    recondition battleship <hi rendition="#italic">Yavuz</hi> (mytel 1157, November 1<anchor n="48"
                        xml:id="d723-f1"/>) and indications we shall probably be asked consider
                    credits for modernizing Turkey’s military defenses, I wish to revise
                    recommendations in my 1157 that we accede to request re <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Yavuz</hi> and in my 1147, October 28, re additional credits. I believe
                    that, before reaching decision on these matters, consideration should be given
                    to such factors as (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) value and utility of
                    reconditioned battleship in relation to other elements Turkey’s defenses for
                    which credits may be needed (this may require <pb facs="0930" n="918"
                        xml:id="pg918"/> study and advice by General Staff), (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) Turkey’s essential economic development projects
                    (this may require expert economic study and advice) and (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >c</hi>) independent assessment of extent to which Turkey’s assets and
                    revenues could contribute to financing above needs (Lawson<anchor n="49"
                        xml:id="d723-f2"/> has already begun study of this point and is exchanging
                    views informally with British colleague).</p>
                <p>What strikes me about these various requests for financial aid is that they lack
                    coordination and planning. Consideration of Turkey’s essential needs in fields
                    economic development and national defense in light of whatever credits we and
                    British may be able to furnish with resultant establishment priorities would
                    seem important if Turkey is to derive maximum benefit from outside
                    assistance.</p>
                <p>I should appreciate Department’s views.<anchor n="50" xml:id="d723-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d723-f1" n="48" xml:id="d723-f1-content">
                <p>See footnote 47, above.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d723-f2" n="49" xml:id="d723-f2-content">
                <p>Edward B. Lawson, Counselor of Embassy for Economic Affairs in Turkey.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d723-f3" n="50" xml:id="d723-f3-content">
                <p>In telegram 750, November 13, 7 p.m., to Ankara, the Department expressed full
                    agreement with the Ambassador’s views, reported that the Navy Department has
                    suspended action on the <hi rendition="#italic">Yavuz</hi> case, said Turkey’s
                    economic development and problems of national defense were under active
                    consideration in Washington, and requested an appraisal of Turkey’s heavy burden
                    of military expenditures (867.51/11–1046).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
